{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794997651,"nonce":"6KYCdDCfGwkAPvbSiNbL8CBOnzo=","title":"The PARDISO non-finite-operator test no longer reaches a non-finite operator and fails on main","message":"`tests/test_pardiso_backend_abort.py::TestTheBackendDoesNotKillTheProcess::test_a_non_finite_operator_raises_instead_of_aborting` fails on `main`: the subprocess with the filtered density prints `SURVIVED` and exits 0, so the operator never goes non-finite and the guard's `FloatingPointError` is never raised. The test is marked `slow`, so the default selection does not run it.\n\nThe reproduction has drifted: the optimisation that used to drive the density to NaN after a few steps now stays finite. The guard itself (`_pardiso_host_solve` refuses a non-finite system) is not exercised by any other test.\n\nWanted: a direct test of the guard (a BCOO with a NaN value handed to `pardiso_lu_solve` raises `FloatingPointError` and the process survives), and the end-to-end reproduction either repaired or removed.\n\nPossibly related, not confirmed: a PARDISO factorization entered with a thread count other than the one of its analysis returned wrong values and corrupted the heap. The original abort needed a compiled adjoint next to un-compiled solves, which is the situation that defect needs; the NaN seen then may have been its consequence, not its cause.","files":null}]}